hey guys Greg knuckles here for string  theory calm string with an E and J th  string calm and today we are talking  about work capacity first off what is  work capacity in essence it is the  maximum amount of work that you can do  in the gym and recover from and respond  positively to now why is it important  because not to overstate things it is  the cornerstone of long term progress in  the gym  overtime you simply have to do more work  to continue getting results it's a very  basic principle of exercise physiology  known as the repeated bouts effect that  when you do the same thing over and over  and over your body adapts to it and  essentially doesn't respond as robustly  to it as it once did so over time you  simply have to do more to keep making  progress and that's why we're capacity  is so important because the amount of  work you can maximally do right now is  probably not as much work as you would  need to do to reach your long-term  potential so essentially it's a ceiling  on the amount of work you can do so if  your ceilings here now that's going to  cap how much progress you can make so  you need to raise that ceiling over time  to keep getting bigger and stronger so  that dovetails into a discussion about  plateaus essentially what happens with a  plateau is you're working out you're  doing basically the same stuff weekend  in week out you're making progress for a  while and then you hit a wall and stop  making progress and that pretty much  happens because the amount of work you  need to do to keep making progress is  more than the amount of work you're  currently doing so the simplest way to  keep moving forward is simply to do more  work I've written about that before I'll  link the article in the description so  anyway you need to do more work so that  works for a while as long as the amount  of work you need to do to keep moving  forward is Bo  hello your work capacity the ceiling of  the amount of work you can do so you  know you hit a plateau you do more work  you make more progress you have another  plateau you do more work you make more  progress and that's all well and good  until the amount of work you need to do  is bumping up against your work capacity  and what happens at that point is since  work capacity is the maximal amount you  can do and recover from and adapt  positively to if you end up needing to  do more than that to make progress then  when you increase your workload above  that threshold essentially you can't  recover from it and respond positively  to it anymore and that's when  overreaching or overtraining starts to  set in so from that point the only way  forward is to increase your work  capacity so that's where your aerobic  system comes in because it's the main  driver of how much energy your body can  produce to fuel that work so if you know  let's say you're doing sets of five or  so and it takes you five minutes to  recover between sets but you only have  you know an hour to to work out and the  amount of work you need to do to keep  moving forward is greater than the  amount of work you can cram into that  time period because you're so gassed  after sets then acutely your aerobic  metabolism the amount of energy your  aerobic system can produce is limiting  your work capacity and how much training  you can do there are also chronic  limiters to work capacity and they  mainly have to do with how well you can  recover between workouts a big one is  how resilient your body is to muscle  damage the more you train the less  muscle damage is caused by a given  workload so your body has to do less to  recover from that and kind of on the  flip side of that is how efficiently  your body can synthesize the protein  necessary to recover from a given  workload also your muscles and tendons  resiliency to a given level and amount  of loading that you know essentially  pushes that increases the amount of work  you can do before your muscles and  tendons and joints start feeling beaten  up by the amount of training you have to  do to keep moving forward and another  thing for recovery between sessions is  your autonomic nervous system the more  parasympathetic you are that's your rest  in digest system as opposed to  sympathetic that's your item flight  fight or flight system the more  parasympathetic you are the more robust  of a recovery state your body is in  between workouts finally we're going to  talk about how to increase work capacity  a lot of this just has to do with basic  smart periodization if you've been  training pretty similarly for a long  period of time you've been increasing  your volume you finally hit a plateau  and your lifts aren't going anywhere  you're not getting any bigger or any  stronger then what you have to do is if  you're using block periodization terms  cycle back and do what would be called  an accumulation block or if you aren't  big on terminology basically just doing  more work and lowering the weights  you're working with well the average  weights you're working with there are  two ways to accomplish this number one  is simply to keep training with the same  basic setup you are now but to decrease  training weights anywhere from 5 to 15  percent and increase the amount of total  reps you're doing in a workout so you're  handling a larger training volume the  other way is to shift the emphasis of  your training and to make it less  specific as to the lifts you're trying  to increase so if you know 60 70 percent  of your training now is just squat bench  and deadlift or variations thereof you  can flip that around to maybe make that  20 30 percent of your training being the  main lifts and still keep training them  heavy  but then put a greater emphasis on  accessory work that allows you to handle  a higher total training volume in terms  of implementation that phase would last  anywhere from two to four months  depending on your level as a lifter for  someone who's more of a beginner  intermediate hitting their first major  plateaus that might be two months for  someone in more advanced that might be  three four months maybe even a little  bit longer and now during that period  your goal should still be to progress in  a measurable fashion except typically  you would be progressing by trying to  increase the amount of weights you have  on the bar but during that period you  would be trying to progress by  increasing your weekly workload week to  week and just as a general rule of thumb  of five to ten percent increase in  workload week to week is a good place to  start so over three months twelve weeks  of training that would be increasing  your workload roughly 60 to 120 percent  and doubling your workload that may  sound like a lot but when you really get  into it it's it's not nearly as bad as  it sounds especially because you're  decreasing the loading of it and finally  it may be necessary to increase the  amount of cardiovascular work you're  doing simply to increase the aerobic  base to fuel this type of training and  for that I would recommend basic low  intensity steady-state cardio interval  training seems to be in vogue lately but  during this period remember what you're  trying to do is increase the stress on  your body to long term increase the  amount of stress it can handle and  things like sprints those are other very  stressful that's another very stressful  exercise both for your muscles and your  nervous system so you'd be better served  there just a incline treadmill walk low  intensity steady-state on a bike trying  to keep your heart rate 125 to 140 for  25 to 60 minutes or so two to three  times a week um  that will help improve your aerobic  conditioning without significantly  limiting your ability to recover between  training sessions so that's all for  today guys hope you enjoyed it if you  did please like and subscribe check out  my website string theory calm and jth  strength calm or I'm the Content Manager  and also please check out the link in  